# Chapter 6, first part: the map of printing, the Print menu, Draft, Final,
# sending to a printer, the messages and the place of the print errors.
# Included by the chapter file; headlines of level 2 and below only.

.h2 How printing works in Strata [print-overview]
.ix Printing, overview
.ix Typesetting
.ix CUPS, lp
.ix Page breaks
Strata typesets the pages of a document and, when a print queue is named,
hands the result to the printer of the system.  Four pieces take part.
.table 13 47
Piece | Task
The Editor | Owns the Print menu.  The part of the document that you ask for is printed, a wait panel is shown while the print runs and the result is reported.
The Printer | The typesetter, which turns a document into a PDF file, or into
 the pages of a Preview ({n:print-strprint}).
The Viewer | Shows the pages of a Preview in a window ({n:print-dviview}).
{c:lp} | The print command of the system.  The Editor uses it when the Printer
 field of a panel names a queue.
.end
The command determines the result.
.table 16 14 20
You want | Use | Result
A PDF file of the document | {k:F12}, Print, Final ({k:Ctrl-P}) with Printer
 | File only | The PDF named in Output File
A quick PDF to read through | {k:F12}, Print, Draft | A one-spaced PDF with
 the comments printed and the pictures in color
Paper | Final or Draft with a queue in Printer | {c:lp} sends the pages to the
 queue; no PDF file is kept
The print dialog of a program that shows PDF files | Final or Draft with PDF
 viewer in Printer | The PDF, then a program of the machine opens it
A look at the pages on the screen | {k:F12}, Print, Preview (Document, Block,
 | Section) | The Viewer shows the typeset pages in a window
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: a file made from a shell or a script comes from the print mode
of the Editor ({n:print-cli}); it gives the PDF, a line of text and an exit
status.
.endif
{b:Draft} and {b:Final} make their pages the same way.  They differ in the
settings that they use and in the fields of the panel ({n:print-draft} and
{n:print-final}).  With File only or PDF viewer the PDF is the product: it is
the Output File, and it can be printed again later without making it again.
With a queue the pages are made, sent and deleted; no file is written next to
the document and nothing puts the question whether to replace one.  {X:print-memory}.

The pages are real pages: justified text with hyphenation, the margins and the
paper size of the document, footnotes at the foot of the page, tables,
pictures, equations and keycaps.  The page breaks and the line breaks are
those of the typeset page, so the pages of a printed document are the pages
of the PDF.  Printing and Preview need a TeX system on the machine
({n:print-prereq}).

What Strata has no marker for can still be printed: a LaTeX marker hands its
text to the typesetter exactly as it was typed ({n:marker-latex}).

.h2 The Print menu [print-menu]
.ix Print menu, entries
.ix F12 menu bar, Print
.ix Ctrl-P
.ix Menu path, Print
.ix Draft, Final, Preview
.ix Hint line, Print menu
The Print menu is the fourth entry of the menu bar, after File, Global and
Edit and before Tools.  It has seven entries: three for printing and four for
the errors that a print finds.
The hint line of the menu bar shows the hint of the highlighted entry.

.h3 How to reach it
.table 20 40
Way | What it does
{k:F12}, then {k:P} | Opens the menu bar and the Print menu.  The letter
 chooses the entry by its first letter.
{k:F12}, {k:Right} or {k:Left} to Print, {k:Enter} | The same, with the cursor
 keys.
{k:Ctrl-P} | Opens the Print Document panel directly: the same as Print,
 Final.
.end
.pic pictures/print-menu.png 5.4 The Print menu with the hint line of Draft.

.h3 The entries
.table 10 8 8 24
Entry | Letter | State | What it does
Draft | {k:D} | Works | Makes a quick PDF and sends it to a printer if one is
 named ({n:print-draft}).
Final | {k:F} | Works | Makes the PDF with all that the document has, with
 title page, copies and ranges, and sends it to a printer if one is named
 ({n:print-final}).
Preview | {k:P} | Works | A menu of three: Document, Block, Section ({n:print-preview}).
Next Error | (cursor keys) | Works | Finds the next Print Error marker, as
 {k:Ctrl-F8} does ({n:edit-c-find-error}).
Previous Error | (cursor keys) | Works | Finds the previous one, as {k:Ctrl-F7}
 does.
Remove Error | (cursor keys) | Works | Takes out the Print Error marker under
 the cursor.
Remove Print Errors | (cursor keys) | Works | Takes out every Print Error
 marker.
.end
The last four entries are reached with {k:Right} and {k:Enter}.  The row of
the seven entries is wider than 80 columns: on a screen of 80 columns the last
entry, Remove Print Errors, is on a second row, and the hint line is one row
lower.  The markers are described in {x:print-errors}.
When an entry is highlighted the hint line shows its text: "Print document in
draft quality", "Print document in final quality" and "Document, Block,
Section" for Preview.  The three entries of the Preview menu have "Preview
entire document", "Preview the current block" and "Preview the current
section".  The hints of the last four are "Find the next Print Error
[Ctrl-F8]", "Find the previous Print Error [Ctrl-F7]", "Remove the Print Error
under the cursor" and "Remove all the Print Errors".

.h3 Esc and the other ways out
{k:Esc} closes the menu one level at a time.  A panel is left with {k:Esc},
which changes nothing, or accepted with {k:Ins}; the keys of the panels are
those of {x:view-form-keys}.  Nothing is made until a panel is
accepted.

.h3 Print and Preview with no document open
.ix Document Manager, in the Editor
.ix Editor without a file
The Editor with no file open shows a small Document
Manager menu before it opens a document ({n:str-inside}).  Open a document
with its entry Edit / Preview / Print Document; Print and Preview are then
on the Print menu of the Editor.

.h2 Draft [print-draft]
.ix Draft
.ix Draft Print panel
.ix Print Current Section Only?
.ix Print Double Spaced?
.ix Output File
.ix Printer, field of the Draft panel
.ix F12, Print, Draft
Draft is the quick print.  It makes the PDF with the same engine as Final,
but in the way of a proof: one line spacing whatever the document says, no
extra space between blocks, and the comments of the document printed
with their author and time; the pictures are printed as in Final.  Comments are printed in a Draft only.
A comment is printed in small typewriter type, in square brackets, as {c:[Comment
by Ada Example, 2026-05-02 09:15: Check the date.]}.  Nothing else of the page
is different from a Final.  The sample {c:sample-comments} has four comments: print
it as a Draft and as a Final to see the difference.  It is in the directory
{c:share/strata/samples/comments} of the directory where Strata is installed.

.h3 How to reach it
{k:F12}, {k:P}, {k:D}.  The menu path is Print, Draft.

.pic pictures/print-draft-panel.png 5.4 The Draft Print panel.

.h3 The panel
The panel is titled {b:Draft Print}.  {k:Down}, {k:Tab} or {k:Enter} go to the
next field and {k:Up} to the previous one; {k:Ins} accepts the panel and
{k:Esc} cancels it.  The fields are, in order, these.
.table 14 12 24
Field | Values | Meaning
Output File | A file name | The PDF that is made.  The default is the
 document's name with its extension replaced by {c:-draft.pdf}, in the document's
 directory ({c:report.doc} gives {c:report-draft.pdf}), so that a Draft does not
 replace the final PDF.  When Setup names a PDF Directory that can be used,
 the file is in that directory instead; if it cannot be used (it does not
 exist, it cannot be written, or it is a relative path) the document's
 directory is used and the message line reports this.  A name that you type is
 kept as you typed it.  A document with no name gives {c:Untitled-draft.pdf} in
 the directory where the Editor was started.  A name without a directory is in
 that working directory.  {k:F2} or {k:Keypad +} in the field lists the PDF
 files.  The field is inactive when Printer is a queue, because a queue keeps
 no file: it stays in its place with its text dim, the cursor skips it
 and nothing can be typed in it.  File only and PDF viewer make it active
 again, with the text it had.
Printer | File only, PDF viewer, a queue, System default printer | Where to
 send the PDF.  A choice field: {k:Space} steps through the entries and goes
 round from the last to the first, and the field takes no typed text.
 File only makes the PDF and stops.  PDF viewer makes
 the PDF and opens it in a program of the machine that shows PDF files, so
 that you print it from the dialog of that program.  System default printer
 sends the PDF to the queue that the system uses when none is named.  The
 default follows Print To and Printer Queue of the Document Manager's Setup
 ({n:str-setup-fields}); without them it is File only.  {k:F2} or
 {k:Keypad +} lists File only, PDF viewer, the queues of the system and
 System default printer.  {X:print-sending}.
Copies | 1 to 99 | The number of copies, given to {c:lp}.  Default 1.  More than
 one copy needs a queue.
Print Double Sided? | Printer default, Yes, No | Yes requests two-sided output from {c:lp} on the long edge, No one-sided, Printer default gives {c:lp} no
 option.  Needs a queue.
Sort Output Pages? | Printer default, Yes, No | Yes has {c:lp} collate copies
 (pages 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3 for three copies), No not to (1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2).
 Means something only for more than one copy.  Needs a queue.
Print Current Section Only? | No (default), Yes | Yes makes the section the
 cursor is in, with all the sections below it.  In an unstructured document
 the part is the block the cursor is in.
Print Double Spaced? | No (default), Yes | Yes doubles the line spacing of
 every block.  It changes the PDF, so it works with File only too.
Print from Page | A page number, or empty | The first page for {c:lp}.  A blank
 field is page 1.  Needs a queue.
to Page | A page number, or empty | The last page for {c:lp}.  A blank field is
 the last page of the PDF.  Needs a queue.
Error Log? | Yes (default), No | Yes ends the PDF with the Error Log when the
 document has errors or warnings; No leaves the log out ({n:print-errlog}).
 The errors are marked in the text either way, and the banner and the marks
 of a failed print are on the pages either way ({n:print-failed}).
Table of Contents? | Yes or No, as the document says | Yes puts a page of contents first,
 with the headlines of the document and the page of each
 ({n:print-contents}).  The field starts with the choice that the document
 stores (Global, Format, fourth panel): Yes for a structured document that
 has headlines.  A change is for this print only.
Contents Levels | 1 to 99, as the document says | The deepest level of headline
 that the contents list; 3 is shown when the document stores none.  Used only
 when Table of Contents? is Yes.
Index? | Yes or No, as the document says | Yes makes an index at the end of the PDF from
 the Index markers of the document ({n:print-index}).  The field starts at
 Yes when the document has an Index marker.
List of Figures? | Yes or No, as the document says | Yes puts a list of the Figure markers
 after the contents ({n:print-index}).
List of Pictures? | Yes or No, as the document says | The same for the names of the
 Picture markers.
List of Tables? | Yes or No, as the document says | The same for the Table markers.
Print in Black and White? | No (default), Yes | Yes prints every color of the
 print as a grey, for a printer that has no color ({n:print-colors}).  It
 changes the PDF, so it works with File only too.
Print Status | (shown, not a field) | Present only when {c:lp} is not found: "{c:lp}
 not found: only the PDF is made".
.end
{k:Space}, {k:Left} and {k:Right} change a choice field; the Printer field
is one of them.  The first printing key typed in a text field replaces the
whole value.  A page number is a
whole number from 1 to 99999.

.h3 What happens when the panel is accepted
.ix Replace NAME?
.ix The PDF cannot replace the document
.ix There is no current section
1. The panel is checked in the order of its fields.  The first value that is
 refused shows a box with the reason and leaves the panel open (the
 messages are in {n:print-messages}).
2. If the Output File is shown and exists, the question "Replace NAME?" is
 asked, with the name of the file without its directory.  No returns to the
 panel.  A print to a queue makes no file and puts no question.
3. The part to print is cut out: the section of the cursor with Print
 Current Section Only? at Yes, otherwise the whole document without its
 title page.  A Draft has no title page and no range of sections.  The
 document in the editor is printed as it is in memory, with the changes that
 are not saved; the file of the document is not touched.
4. The wait panel of {x:print-progress} is shown while the Printer
 and then {c:lp} work.
5. The PDF is written to its place only when it is complete, and the old
 file stays as it was until then.  The end message is shown ({n:print-messages}).
If Print Current Section Only? is Yes and the cursor is in no section, a box
titled "Print" says "There is no current section".

.h3 Edges
.table 19 41
Case | What happens
Empty document | A PDF of one empty page.
Cursor on a marker | Nothing special; Print Current Section Only? uses the
 section or block of the cursor.
Unwritable directory | The panel is refused: "Cannot write in the directory
 NAME".
The document itself named as Output File | Refused: "The PDF cannot replace
 the document".
Document with unsaved changes | The text in the editor is printed, with the
 changes.
Block marked | The mark does not matter.
.end

.h2 Final [print-final]
.ix Final
.ix Print Document panel
.ix Print Title Page?
.ix Print from Section
.ix to Section
.ix Section range, print
.ix F12, Print, Final
Final is the print of the best quality that Strata has.  It typesets the
document with all that it has: the line spacing and the spacing of blocks
that the document asks for, the pictures, the title page, and no comments.
It has the fields of Draft and three more: Print Title Page?, Print from
Section and to Section.

.h3 How to reach it
{k:F12}, {k:P}, {k:F}, or {k:Ctrl-P} anywhere in the Editor.  The Document
Manager has no entry for printing: open the document with Edit / Preview /
Print Document ({n:str-edit}) and print it from the Editor.

.pic pictures/print-final-panel.png 5.4 The Print Document panel (Final).

.h3 The panel
The panel is titled {b:Print Document}.  The fields are in this order.
.table 14 12 24
Field | Values | Meaning
Output File | A file name | The PDF that is made.  The default is the
 document's name with {c:.pdf} in the document's directory ({c:plan.doc} gives
 {c:plan.pdf}), or in the PDF Directory of Setup when it can be used.  A document
 with no name gives {c:Untitled.pdf} in the working directory.  The directory must
 exist and be writable, and the PDF cannot replace the document.  {k:F2} or {k:Keypad +} lists the PDF files.
 The field is inactive when Printer is a queue, as for Draft.
Printer | File only, PDF viewer, a queue, System default printer | As for
 Draft: a choice field.
Copies | 1 to 99 | As for Draft.
Print Double Sided? | Printer default, Yes, No | As for Draft.
Sort Output Pages? | Printer default, Yes, No | As for Draft.
Print Current Section Only? | No (default), Yes | Yes makes the section of the
 cursor with its subsections (the block, in an unstructured document).  With
 Yes the title page and the range of sections are not used.
Print Title Page? | Yes (default), No | Yes makes the blocks before the first
 level 1 section (the title page) the first page.  No leaves them out.  Only
 the whole document has a title page: with Print Current Section Only? at Yes,
 or with a range, none is made.
Print Double Spaced? | No (default), Yes | Doubles the line spacing of every
 block.
Print from Section | A section number, or empty | The first section to print,
 such as 2 or 1.2.  Empty means the first section.
to Section | A section number, or empty | The last section to print; the
 section is printed with its subsections.  Empty means to the end of the
 document.
Print from Page | A page number, or empty | As for Draft.
to Page | A page number, or empty | As for Draft.
Error Log? | Yes (default), No | As for Draft ({n:print-errlog}).
Table of Contents? | Yes or No, as the document says | As for Draft; the contents come
 after the title page ({n:print-contents}).
Contents Levels | 1 to 99, as the document says | As for Draft.
Index? | Yes or No, as the document says | As for Draft ({n:print-index}).
List of Figures? | Yes or No, as the document says | As for Draft.
List of Pictures? | Yes or No, as the document says | As for Draft.
List of Tables? | Yes or No, as the document says | As for Draft.
Print in Black and White? | No (default), Yes | As for Draft ({n:print-colors}).
Print Status | (shown, not a field) | As for Draft.
.end

.h3 The table of contents [print-contents]
.ix Table of contents
.ix Contents, table of
.ix Table of Contents?
.ix Contents Levels
A table of contents is a page that lists the headlines of the document.  It is
made when the document is printed, from its headlines, and it is not in the
text: a structured document that has a headline has it with nothing to set,
because the document stores the level of its contents (Table of Contents Level
on the fourth Global Format panel, {n:format-fields-lists}; 99 when it stores
none, 0 for no contents).  Table of Contents? in the panel starts with that
choice and a change of it is for one print.  The PDF has a page of contents
after the title page (in a Draft, which has no title page, it is the first
page).  Each line
has the tag of the headline as the screen shows it, the title, a row of dots
and the page on which the headline begins: a chapter appears as
{c:1 Introduction . . . . . 1} and a section of the next level, indented, as
{c:1.2 The scope . . . . . 3}.
Contents Levels says how deep the list goes: 1 lists the chapters, 2 the
chapters and their sections, and so on up to 99; a document that stores no
level lists every level, and the field of the panel then shows 3.  A headline
of a deeper level is in the text and not in the list.  The indent of the lines
stops at the ninth level, and a tag of more than 30 characters, the tag of a
section nested 15 levels deep or more, is broken after a point in the column
of the tags.  The pages of the
contents are numbered i, ii and so on, and the body starts again at page 1,
so that the page numbers in the list are the page numbers of the body.  The
page numbers are found when the document is typeset, which takes the
Printer a second run; nothing is to be done for it.
.h4 What it lists
- Only the headlines of a structured document are listed.  A document with no
 headline, or an unstructured one, has no contents page unless Table of
 Contents? is set to Yes; the page then has one line that says so.
- Print Current Section Only? and a range of sections list only the sections
 that are printed, with the numbers that the screen shows, and the pages of
 that print.
- The title of a headline is its text; a picture, an equation or another
 marker in it is left out of the list, except a LaTeX marker such as the logo
 of a name, which is set as it is in the headline.
- A Preview makes the contents page, as a Print does, from the settings of the
 document.
- The layout panel Table of Contents Page Layout of Global, Page does not
 change this page: it has the margins of the other pages.

.h3 The index and the lists [print-index]
.ix Index?
.ix List of Figures?
.ix List of Tables?
.ix Index, printed
.ix List of figures, printed
.ix List of tables, printed
The Index, Figure, Picture and Table markers of a document ({n:marker-index},
{n:marker-figure}, {n:marker-picture}) make an index and three lists when the
document is printed: the list of figures from the Figure markers, the list of
pictures from the names of the Picture markers, the list of tables from the
Table markers.  Each of the four is {b:Automatic} unless the document says
otherwise: it is made when the document has at least one marker of its kind,
and not otherwise.  The document counts its markers as they are made and
taken out, so a list turns on with the first marker and off with the last
(Global, Format, fourth panel, {n:format-fields-lists}, shows the counts).  The
choice of each list can be made On or Off there, and it is stored in the
document.  Off keeps the list away although the document has markers; On with
no marker makes nothing, because a list with no entry is not printed.  The
fields Index?, List of Figures?, List of Pictures? and List of Tables? of the
panels start with what the document says, and a change is for one print.  A
marker whose list is made prints nothing at its place; when its list is off it
is printed there as text.

The index is at the end of the PDF, on a page of its own.  Its entries are the
Main Headings, in alphabetical order with the case ignored; the entries that
have a Subheading are under their main heading, indented, in alphabetical
order too.  After each heading comes the page of every marker that has it,
such as {c:Strata, 3, 7}; three pages or more that follow each other are
written as a run, {c:Strata, 3-5} (with an en dash).  A Reference Text takes the
place of the
pages: {c:Map, See Strata}.  A marker with no main heading makes no entry.  The
index has no winding columns.

The lists are after the table of contents, on pages numbered i, ii and so on,
each list on a page of its own: first the list of figures, with the name of
every Figure marker, then the list of pictures, with the name of every Picture
marker that has one, then the list of tables.
Each line is the name, a row of dots and the page.  A document that has no
marker of a kind gets no page for it.  The pages are
found when the document is typeset, in a second run, as for the contents.  A
print of a master document ({n:marker-include}) makes the index and the lists
of the whole work.
.pic pictures/print-index-page.png 5 The index, the last page of the print.
.pic pictures/print-lists-page.png 5 The list of figures, with the page of each figure.

.h3 The range of sections
.ix Section number, in a panel
.ix Range of sections
A section number is the one shown on the screen: 1, 2.1, 2.1.2.  It is written
as the tag appears in the text, with no trailing dot.  Only a structured
document has sections.
.table 20 40
You enter | The part printed
Both empty | The whole document (with the title page when Print Title Page? is
 Yes).
from 2.1, to empty | From the start of section 2.1 to the end of the document.
from empty, to 2 | From the start of the document, with no title page, to the
 end of section 2 (all its subsections included).
from 2.1, to 2.1 | Section 2.1 and its subsections.
from 3, to 2 | Refused: "The first section is after the last".
from 9.9 | Refused: "Cannot find section number 9.9".
.end
A part that is cut out of the document keeps the numbers that its sections
have on the screen: section 2.1 is printed as 2.1, with its subsections 2.1.1
and so on.  The same is true of Preview ({n:print-preview}) and of Print
Current Section Only?.  The page numbers of the part start at 1.

.h3 Pages: a range for the printer
{b:Print from Page} and {b:to Page} do not cut the PDF.  The PDF has all the
pages, so that it can be printed again; the range goes to {c:lp} with its {c:-P}
option when the file is sent ({n:print-sending}).  A "from" page
after the end of the PDF is refused when the PDF is made: the PDF is kept
and the message shows "The document has only 2 pages, so it has no page 5".

.h3 What the panels keep [print-memory]
.ix Print panel, remembered choices
.ix Remembered printer
After a print that is not cancelled, the Print panels (Draft and Final) keep
the choices that are about the printer: Printer, Print Double Sided?, Sort
Output Pages? and Print in Black and White?.  The next panel in the same
session starts with them, and so does the next session: they are stored in the
settings ({n:appcfg-format}, the keys {c:print_last_printer},
{c:print_last_sides}, {c:print_last_sort} and {c:print_last_bw}).  The first
time, and until something was printed, the panel starts with the Print To and
Printer Queue of Setup.  A panel that is cancelled keeps nothing.
.table 22 38
Not kept | Why
Output File | It belongs to this document and this print.
Copies | It is 1 each time: the number of copies belongs to one job.
Print from Page, to Page, the sections, Print Current Section Only? | They
 belong to this print.
Print Title Page?, Error Log?, Double Spaced?, the contents and the lists |
 They belong to the document or to this print; the document's own choices are
 read from the document first.
.end
When the kept printer is a queue that the system no longer reports, the field
shows File only and the message line reports this; the panel opens as usual.  Two
sessions that print at the same time: the one that writes last is kept.

.h2 Colors in print [print-colors]
.ix Colors in print
.ix Print in Black and White?
.ix Black and white print
.ix Gray print
A document holds no color: nothing in it states that a headline, a block, a
marker or a border is red or blue, so a print has no color of that kind to
show.  The colors of a print are the fixed ones that the Printer uses: the
marks of the Print Error markers (red for an error, orange for a warning), the
banner of a failed print ({n:print-failed}), the shades of grey of a keycap and
the colors that a picture or an equation has of its own.  The colors of the
screen that {b:Setup} lets you choose ({n:str-colours}) are for the screen only
and are never printed.
The field {b:Print in Black and White?} of the Draft and Final panels turns
every color of the print, and of a Preview, into a grey of the same brightness
(red becomes a dark grey, orange a lighter one), so that a printer with no
color makes a clear page; a picture is printed in greys (a Preview keeps the
colors of its pictures).  With No, the default, the print has its colors,
and a picture that has color is printed in color.  A print in black and white of a document that has
nothing in color (no error, no banner) is the same print.  A Draft and a Final
print have the same rule.  The same choice for the command line is
{c:--black-and-white}.

.h2 Sending to a printer [print-sending]
.ix lp
.ix lpstat
.ix Printer field, queue
.ix File only
.ix PDF viewer
.ix PDF, open to print
.ix Print queue
.ix lp, options
The Printer field is a choice field.  Its entries are File only, PDF viewer,
each queue that CUPS knows, System default printer and Search for printers...
({n:print-search}); it takes no typed text.  When a queue is chosen, the Editor sends the PDF to it with {c:lp} as
soon as the PDF is whole.  With PDF viewer the Editor opens the PDF instead.
.table 20 40
Printer field | What happens
File only | The PDF is made and the print
 stops.  Copies, Print Double Sided?, Sort Output Pages? and the page range
 have nothing to act on, so they are rejected: "More than one copy needs
 a printer: name one in Printer" (the first words are "A page range", "Double
 sided" or "Sort output pages" for the others).  Print Double Spaced? works,
 because it changes the PDF.
PDF viewer | The PDF is made and the Editor opens
 it in a program of the machine that shows PDF files, so that you print it
 from the dialog of that program or save it from there.  The Editor does not
 wait for the program.  The message line shows where the PDF is and that it was
 opened.  Copies, Print Double Sided?, Sort Output Pages? and the page range
 are refused as they are for File only, because the dialog of the program has
 its own: "More than one copy needs a printer: name one in Printer (the
 program that opens the PDF has its own print dialog)".  Print Double Spaced?
 works.  The setting {c:pdf_viewer} ({x:appcfg-key-pdf-viewer}) names another
 program.  If no program that shows PDF files is found, or the machine has no
 display, the PDF is saved and the message line reports this.
A queue name | The PDF is made and sent to that queue.
A queue name from the settings that the system does not report | Kept as the
 value and shown as an entry; the PDF is sent to it.
A name that is not a queue name (given on the command line, or in an old
 settings file): a space, a slash, a quote, a comma, a backslash, a number
 sign, a first minus or a character that is not printable ASCII | Refused:
 "NAME is not the name of a printer queue".
.end
The default of the Printer field follows Print To and Printer Queue of Setup
in the Document Manager ({n:str-setup-fields}): File only, PDF viewer, the
queue, or System default printer for the queue that the system uses when none
is named; without them it is File only.  When Print To is Printer and the
system has no queue, the field is File only and the message line shows that no
printer is available.  The entries are File only, PDF viewer, the queues that
the command {c:lpstat -a} gives, System default printer and, last, Search for
printers...  A queue that the settings name and the system does not report is
an entry too, and is kept.
.table 17 43
Key | What it does
{k:Space} | The next entry; after the last entry, File only.
{k:Right}, {k:Left} | The next and the previous entry; they stop at the ends.
{k:Keypad +}, {k:F2} | List the entries; {k:Enter} chooses one.
.end
The list is read from the system in the background when the Editor starts and
again, in the background, when what it holds is older than 30 seconds, so a
queue that you add while the Editor runs shows at the next opening of a
panel.  A machine with no queues shows File only, PDF viewer and Search for
printers... alone.

.h3 Searching for printers [print-search]
.ix Search for printers
.ix Printer field, search for printers
.ix lpinfo
.ix lpadmin
Step to Search for printers..., the last entry of the Printer field, with
{k:Space} (or choose it in the list of {k:F2}) and press {k:Enter}, or accept
the panel with {k:Ins}, to find a printer that has no queue yet.  The panel
stays open and the field goes back to the entry that it had before.  The Editor
runs {c:lpinfo -v}, which lists the devices that CUPS can reach, for at most
ten seconds while a message reports this; {k:Esc} stops it at once.  The result
is a list with each device of the network or of a USB port: its kind, its
address (for example {c:socket://192.168.1.201:9100}), its make and model
when the address has one, and the command that adds it, for example
.table 26 34
Device | Command
A device that CUPS can use without a driver ({c:ipp}, {c:ipps},
 {c:dnssd}) | {c:sudo lpadmin -p NAME -E -v ADDRESS -m everywhere}
A {c:socket}, {c:lpd} or USB device | {c:sudo lpadmin -p NAME -E -v ADDRESS
 -m DRIVER}, with the hint to find DRIVER in the list that {c:lpinfo -m}
 prints
.end
The list shows the full path of {c:lpadmin} and {c:lpinfo}, because the directory
of the programs for the administration of the system is not in the search
path of an ordinary user on some systems.
No queue is added by the Editor, and {c:lpadmin} or {c:sudo} is never run: the
text only shows what to type in a terminal.  When nothing is found the list
shows that the printer must be switched on and be on the same network as
your machine; when {c:lpinfo} fails, for example because your user is not in
the lpadmin group, the list shows its message.  The field keeps its value:
Search for printers... is never the printer that a print goes to.
After you add the queue, open the panel again: the queue is in the list.

.h3 The options given to lp
.table 26 34
Field | Option of {c:lp}
Printer | {c:-d QUEUE} (no {c:-d} for System default printer)
Copies | {c:-n COPIES} (always given)
Print Double Sided?  Yes | {c:-o sides=two-sided-long-edge}
Print Double Sided?  No | {c:-o sides=one-sided}
Sort Output Pages?  Yes | {c:-o collate=true}
Sort Output Pages?  No | {c:-o collate=false}
Print from Page, to Page | {c:-P FROM-TO} (a blank from is 1, a blank to is the
 last page of the PDF)
A page that is an envelope | {c:-o print-scaling=none}, so that the print system
 does not scale the page ({n:format-page-paper})
the name of the document | {c:-t NAME} (only when the document has a name)
.end
The last argument is the full path of the PDF.  Printer default gives no
option, and the queue's own default is used.

.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h3 Environment
.ix STRATA_LP
.ix STRATA_LPSTAT
.table 15 45
Environment variable | Meaning
{c:STRATA_LP} | The program to run in place of {c:lp} (for a site that has another
 print command, and for tests).  Without it {c:lp} is looked for in the system directories.
{c:STRATA_LPSTAT} | The program to run in place of {c:lpstat} for the list of queues.
{c:STRATA_LPINFO} | The program to run in place of {c:lpinfo} for the search for
 printers.
.end
For developers: the environment variable {c:STRATA_STRPRINT} names the Printer
to
use in place of the one next to the Editor or in the system directories.
.endif

.h2 While a print runs [print-progress]
.ix Wait panel
.ix Print cancelled
.ix Esc, in a print
The Editor does not freeze while a print runs.  A wait panel, like the one of
Preview, shows the stage and the progress.
.table 26 34
Stage line | Meaning
Writing | The part of the document is being prepared.
Running LaTeX (pass N) | The pages are being made.  A document that refers to
 page numbers needs another round; the panel then shows "Another pass, for the
 references."
Page N of about M | The page now being made, and an estimate of the total.
 When the total is exact the panel shows "Page N of M".
Sending to the printer | {c:lp} is running.  The hint "Esc stops lp" is shown.
.end
A gauge with the percentage is shown under the stage.  {k:Esc} cancels:
the print is stopped, nothing is written (a PDF that was there before stays
as it was) and the message line shows "Print cancelled".  {k:Esc} while {c:lp} is
running stops {c:lp}; the PDF was already made, and a box reports this and shows that
the job may or may not have been queued.

.h2 The messages of a print [print-messages]
.ix Messages, Print
.ix Wrote
.ix LaTeX failed
.ix lp failed
.ix Print cancelled, message
A good print ends with a message on the message line when nothing was sent,
and with a box when {c:lp} was used, so that the answer of {c:lp} can be read in
full.  A refused value shows a box of the panel; the panel stays open.  The
messages that name a program of Strata are shown here with three dots in
the place of its name.
.table 31 29
Message | Meaning
Wrote /work/plan.pdf (12 pages) | The PDF is made and not sent.  The file
 name is the full path.
PRINT FAILED: Wrote /work/plan.pdf (12 pages).  3 errors and 1 warning. |
 The PDF is made and not sent, and the document has errors: the print FAILED,
 and every page has the banner and the marks ({n:print-failed}).  The text
 goes on with the count; {x:print-errline} has the other forms.
Wrote /work/plan.pdf (12 pages) and sent it to laser (request id is laser-17
 (1 file(s))) | The PDF is made and {c:lp} took it.  The text in the last brackets
 is the answer of {c:lp}.  Only a print that is asked to keep its PDF says
 this.
Sent to laser: 12 pages, 2 copies, double sided, sorted.  No PDF was kept |
 A print to a queue: {c:lp} took the pages, and the PDF that was made for it
 is deleted.  The line shows the copies, the sides and the order that were sent.
{c:lp} failed (status N): TEXT  Nothing was sent and no PDF was kept: choose
 File only to keep one. | {c:lp} ended with an error (TEXT is its own message) and
 the PDF was deleted.  Choose File only to make the file, then print it with
 another command.
Print cancelled | {k:Esc} was pressed before {c:lp} ran.
Cancelled while sending to QUEUE.  The PDF FILE was made; the print job may or
 may not have been queued. | {k:Esc} while {c:lp} ran.
Print stopped by a signal | The Editor was stopped from outside, for example
 by a kill command.
.end
Messages of a panel that is refused (the panel stays open).
.table 31 29
Message | Cause
No output file given | Output File is empty.
NAME is a directory | Output File names a directory.
Cannot find the directory DIR | The directory of Output File does not exist.
Cannot write in the directory DIR | The directory cannot be written.
The PDF cannot replace the document | Output File is the document's own path.
Copies must be a number from 1 to 99 | Copies is not a whole number from 1 to
 99.
Contents Levels is a number from 1 to 99 | Table of Contents? is Yes and the
 level is not a whole number from 1 to 99.
A page must be a number from 1 to 99999 | A page field is not a whole number
 in range.
The first page is after the last page | Print from Page is greater than to
 Page.
"NAME" is not the name of a printer queue | See {x:print-sending}.
Search for printers only looks for printers: choose File only, PDF viewer or a
 printer to print | The Printer field holds Search for printers... when the panel
 is accepted ({n:print-search}).
A page range needs a printer: name one in Printer | A page range with File
 only.  The same message form for "More than one copy", "Double sided" and
 "Sort output pages".
Only a structured document has sections | A range of sections for an
 unstructured document.
Cannot find section number NUMBER | A section number that is not in the
 document.
The first section is after the last | The range of sections is empty or turned
 round.
There is no current section | (box titled "Print") Print Current Section Only?
 and the cursor is in no section.
.end
Messages of a print that went wrong (a box).
.table 31 29
Message | Cause
LaTeX failed: ! TEXT l.12 THE LINE | LaTeX stopped.  The first error line of
 the TeX log and the line after it.  No PDF is written.
Print failed: ... TEXT | Any other message of the Printer.
Print needs ...: it was not found next to ... or in the system directories. | The
 Printer is missing.
Print needs LaTeX: {c:pdflatex} was not found in FOLDERS.  See ... | {c:pdflatex} is
 missing ({n:print-prereq}).
Cannot write the PDF: REASON | The PDF could not be put in its place.
Wrote FILE.pdf (N pages) and opened it to print | PDF viewer: the PDF is made
 and a program of the machine that shows PDF files was started.
Wrote FILE.pdf but no PDF viewer was found: set one in Setup: it is saved |
 PDF viewer: there is no such program, or the machine has no display, or
 the setting {c:pdf_viewer} names a word processor, which is refused.  The PDF is
 kept and the print is not a failure.
Wrote FILE.pdf but the program of the setting {c:pdf_viewer} was not found: it is
 saved | The setting names a program that is not there.
Wrote FILE.pdf but the program that opens it could not be started: it is saved
 | The program could not be run.
Wrote FILE.pdf but lp was not found, so it was not sent to QUEUE.  Install
 cups-client. | The PDF is made; {c:lp} is missing.
{c:lp} failed (status N): TEXT  The PDF FILE was made. | {c:lp} ended with an error,
 for example "lp: Error - The printer or class does not exist."  The PDF is
 kept.
The PDF FILE was made but was not sent.  TEXT | The page range does not fit
 the PDF.
The PDF FILE was made but {c:lp} did not answer within a minute. | {c:lp} was stopped
 after a minute.
The PDF FILE was made but {c:lp} could not be started. | {c:lp} could not be run.
.end
